Scott asks that the least family \(S\) on \(\Sigma=\{0,1\}^*\), containing \(\Sigma\) and every singleton and closed under \(XY=\{\sigma\tau\mid\sigma\in X,\tau\in Y\}\) and under nonempty intersection, be a positive neighbourhood system with a computable presentation in the sense of Definition 7.1 (the enumeration \(X_n\), decidable \(X_n\cap X_m=X_k\), and decidable consistency), that \(|S|\) be a semigroup under \(xy=\{Z\in S\mid\exists X\in x\,\exists Y\in y.\,XY\subseteq Z\}\) into which \(\sigma\mapsto\{X\in S\mid\sigma\in X\}\) embeds \(\Sigma\) as a homomorphism, and that the least solutions of both \(\vec\sigma=\sigma\vec\sigma\) and \(\vec\sigma=\vec\sigma\,\sigma\) be tested against \(\vec\sigma\vec\sigma=\vec\sigma\), \(\vec\sigma\vec\sigma\vec\sigma=\vec\sigma\), \(\vec\sigma\vec1\vec\sigma\vec1=\vec\sigma\vec1\), and \(\vec{01}\vec{01}\vec{01}\vec{01}=\vec{01}\vec{01}\). `InS` is that inductive family and `concat` is the split-set product; `Ssys` takes those languages as neighbourhoods with master the set of all bit lists, and `Ssys_isPositive` gets positivity because a member contained in an intersection is nonempty, so the intersection constructor applies. The presentation `SsysX` decodes a Gödel index (`encode`, `decodeFuel`) and sends junk or an empty denotation to \(\Sigma\); `SsysX_mem` and `SsysX_surj` are the two enumeration clauses, `Ssys_cons_computable` decides consistency by the safe-decode criterion `ssys_cons_char_iff`, and `example_722_presentation` is `Ssys_interEq_computable`, deciding intersection-equality by the primitive-recursive characteristic `ssysInterEqChar`. The hint’s decision method is the automaton algebra: `catEps` accepts concatenations, `interDFA` accepts intersections, `exists_accepted_word_short` and `decideEmptyB` decide emptiness by a word shorter than the state set, and `interEqB` decides equality through `diffNFA`. The bundled conclusion `Ssys_partially_effectively_given` is only the consistency presentation `SsysPres`; relation (i) is the separate lemma, so both clauses of Definition 7.1 are proved, while the stronger `ComputablePresentation` (a primitive-recursive intersection witness and a master index) is not assembled. Multiplication is `mulElem`, the displayed filter, and `Basic` proves associativity by taking a neighbourhood of a left-associated product, splitting the concatenated witness, and regrouping with associativity and monotonicity of `concat`; `emb` is the principal filter of neighbourhoods containing the word and `emb_mul` is the homomorphism, singletons one way and monotonicity of concatenation the other. The sidecar of `example_722` names language membership in place of the middle conjunct, but the theorem is `emb_mul`, `mulElem_assoc`, and `streamArrow_mul_self`. The infinite word `streamArrow` is only the least fixed point of `prependMap`, and `streamArrow_eq` is the left equation \(\mathrm{emb}(\sigma)\cdot\vec\sigma=\vec\sigma\). Idempotence `streamArrow_mul_self` is the pair of comparisons `streamArrow_le_mul_self` (the square is a prepend-fixed point, hence above the least one, using associativity) and `streamArrow_mul_self_le` (a neighbourhood of the left factor comes from a finite prepend iterate, which `prependMap_iterElem_mul_streamArrow_le` keeps below \(\vec\sigma\)). `streamArrow_mul_self_self` rewrites the cube twice by that equation, and `streamArrow_containsZero_pow_four` rewrites twice the fourfold product of `streamArrow [false, true]`; `boolNat` sends false to \(0\) and true to \(1\), so that word is \(01\) and the equation is \(\vec{01}^4=\vec{01}^2\). There is no append map, so \(\vec\sigma=\vec\sigma\sigma\) is not formed. `streamArrow_mul_self_append_true` is idempotence of the single left-infinite word `streamArrow (σ ++ [true])`, not the four-factor product \(\vec\sigma\vec1\vec\sigma\vec1\). The filter `streamElem` of neighbourhoods containing every finite power is a different element, and the idempotence argument for it in `Basic` assumes that the power language lies in \(S\). **Theorem:** partial — positivity, both clauses of Definition 7.1, the semigroup law, the embedding, \(\vec\sigma\vec\sigma=\vec\sigma\), the cube, and the \(01\)-power are proved; the right-hand fixed point and \(\vec\sigma\vec1\vec\sigma\vec1=\vec\sigma\vec1\) are not. **Proof:** partial — the square, cube, and \(01\)-power are the least-fixed-point comparisons above, while the third displayed equation is replaced by an instance of the square law and the right-hand equation has no construction.
